--TEST--
Core\SignedCookie::open accepts a cookie while the key that sealed it is anywhere in the ring and refuses it the step after that key leaves, counted over a ring walked one key at a time from both ends — `rule:security/protocol-roster` and `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A bound asserted on both sides. The rotation case next door pins which end of
// the ring `seal` writes with; this one pins what the *rest* of the ring is for,
// which is the half an operator's deploy depends on. The rule
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/signed_cookie.rs` states is membership and nothing
// else — a cookie opens while its key is in the list, at any position, and
// stops the moment it is dropped — so the assertion is a count that moves in
// lockstep with the ring's length rather than one accepted cookie and one
// refused one.
secret bytes $k0 = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
secret bytes $k1 = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
secret bytes $k2 = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
secret bytes $k3 = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
secret bytes $k4 = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $all = [$k0, $k1, $k2, $k3, $k4];

// Five cookies, each sealed under a one-key ring, so each one has exactly one
// key in the whole set that can open it. The one-key ring is the degenerate end
// of the surface and it is where every cookie here comes from.
array<string> $payloads = ["under 0", "under 1", "under 2", "under 3", "under 4"];
array<string> $cookies = [
    Core\SignedCookie::seal($payloads[0], [$k0]),
    Core\SignedCookie::seal($payloads[1], [$k1]),
    Core\SignedCookie::seal($payloads[2], [$k2]),
    Core\SignedCookie::seal($payloads[3], [$k3]),
    Core\SignedCookie::seal($payloads[4], [$k4]),
];

// Growing from the front: the ring `[k0]`, then `[k0, k1]`, and so on. At every
// step the number of cookies that open is the ring's own length — which names
// the last accepted key and the first refused one together on one line. A
// member that stopped one key short of the tail prints plausibly against a
// two-key ring and loses a cookie per rotation in production.
int $j = 1;
while ($j <= 5) {
    array<secret bytes> $ring = Core\Arr::slice($all, 0, $j);
    int $opened = 0;
    int $refused = 0;
    int $c = 0;
    while ($c < 5) {
        try {
            if (Core\SignedCookie::open($cookies[$c], $ring) == $payloads[$c]) {
                $opened = $opened + 1;
            }
        } catch (RuntimeError $outside) {
            $refused = $refused + 1;
        }
        $c = $c + 1;
    }
    echo "a ring of ", $j, " opens ", $opened, " and refuses ", $refused, "\n";
    $j = $j + 1;
}

// And shrinking from the front, which is what retiring a key actually is. This
// half is not the first one read backwards: it walks the *tail* of the list, so
// a key that was the head a moment ago is now at position 0 of a shorter ring
// and every other key has moved. A member that keyed its acceptance off a
// position rather than off membership agrees with the growing walk and
// disagrees here.
int $r = 1;
while ($r <= 4) {
    array<secret bytes> $tail = Core\Arr::slice($all, $r, 5 - $r);
    int $survives = 0;
    int $t = 0;
    while ($t < 5) {
        try {
            if (Core\SignedCookie::open($cookies[$t], $tail) == $payloads[$t]) {
                $survives = $survives + 1;
            }
        } catch (RuntimeError $retired) {
        }
        $t = $t + 1;
    }
    echo "after retiring ", $r, ", ", $survives, " of 5 still open\n";
    $r = $r + 1;
}
--EXPECT--
a ring of 1 opens 1 and refuses 4
a ring of 2 opens 2 and refuses 3
a ring of 3 opens 3 and refuses 2
a ring of 4 opens 4 and refuses 1
a ring of 5 opens 5 and refuses 0
after retiring 1, 4 of 5 still open
after retiring 2, 3 of 5 still open
after retiring 3, 2 of 5 still open
after retiring 4, 1 of 5 still open
